// Per-developer signing configuration.
//
// Setup: copy this file to `signing.xcconfig` (gitignored), put your
// Apple Developer Team ID below, then run `xcodegen` and build.
//
// ─── Finding your Team ID ───
//
// Authoritative source, works for every account type:
//   Xcode → Settings → Accounts → select your Apple ID → the "Team ID"
//   column in the team list.
//
// From the command line, the Team ID is the **OU** field of your signing
// certificate:
//
//   security find-certificate -c "Apple Development" -p \
//     | openssl x509 -noout -subject
//
//   subject=UID=…, CN=Apple Development: you@example.com (AAAAAAAAAA),
//           OU=BBBBBBBBBB, O=Your Name, C=US
//                ^^^^^^^^^^ this is your Team ID
//
// ⚠️  Do NOT use the 10-character string in parentheses after the
// certificate name in `security find-identity` output. For an "Apple
// Development" certificate that parenthetical is a *certificate*
// identifier, not the Team ID — they're different values, and using it
// produces a build that fails with:
//
//   error: No Account for Team "…". Add a new account in Accounts
//   settings or verify that your accounts have valid credentials.
//
// (The parenthetical *is* the Team ID for a "Developer ID Application"
// certificate, which is why this trips people up.)
//
// ─── Why this file exists ───
//
// Use a real team rather than ad-hoc signing ("-"). macOS ties
// Accessibility and Automation grants to the code signature, so ad-hoc
// builds get re-signed on every build and lose their permissions each
// time. A stable team means you grant permissions once.
//
// After editing this file, re-run `xcodegen` — it bakes DEVELOPMENT_TEAM
// into the generated project — then close and reopen the project in Xcode
// so it rereads it.

DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = YOUR_TEAM_ID
